  "sourceId": "2020-P1-Q07",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07140953/TMUA-2020-paper-1.pdf#page=9\">TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 7</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07140955/TMUA-2020-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=9\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 7",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 7 — Determine x+y from two exponential equations.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use one base",
      "body": "<p>Rewrite 4, 8 and 16 as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Powers of 2 have equal values exactly when their exponents are equal.</p>",
      "recap": "Convert to a common base before equating exponents.",
      "pitfall": "The exponent multiplies the whole bracket, including its constant term.",
      "pause": "Rewrite the first equation entirely in base 2.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l05",
          "reminder": "Convert to a common base before equating exponents."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Handle the quotient exponent",
      "body": "<p>The first equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>. The second gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Dividing powers with the same base subtracts their exponents.",
      "pitfall": "Subtract the entire denominator exponent, not only its y-term.",
      "pause": "Simplify the second right-hand side.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l05",
          "reminder": "Dividing powers with the same base subtracts their exponents."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Solve the linear pair",
      "body": "<p>You now have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> into the second equation.</p>",
      "recap": "The exponential equations have become simultaneous linear equations.",
      "pitfall": "Recover both x and y before adding them.",
      "pause": "Find x, then y, and check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> against the options.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l01",
          "reminder": "The exponential equations have become simultaneous linear equations."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The first equation becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. The second becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>. These values satisfy both exponent equations: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Therefore <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></math>, option A.</strong></p>",
